1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,476
Illinois Extension puts it plainly:

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:07,060
lettuce and herbs usually need six
to eight hours of bright light every day.

3
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:10,076
Now look at your kitchen
window in December.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:13,120
How many hours of bright
light does it really give you?

5
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:17,936
That one number decides whether
your winter garden becomes fresh salad

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:22,020
or a tray of pale, stretched out
seedlings leaning toward the glass.

7
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:24,656
Here are seven vegetables.

8
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,356
Five can grow with a bright
window and a few honest limits.

9
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,596
Two of them, tomatoes and peppers,

10
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,236
need extra light in winter,

11
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,440
according to New Hampshire Extension.

12
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,036
And there's one quiet sign,

13
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:38,956
weeks before a plant gives up,

14
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,716
that your window isn't enough.

15
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:42,860
I'll show you exactly where to look.

16
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:44,616
Here's the plan.

17
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,516
First, the light itself,

18
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,996
what a winter window really
gives you and that quiet sign.

19
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,776
Then the seven vegetables,
from the quick ones to the fussy ones.

20
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:57,036
Then one table that lines up
what the universities say,

21
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,720
including three places
where they disagree.

22
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,556
After that, cheap lamps,

23
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:02,976
three common problems,

24
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:04,700
and a four-week plan.

25
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:09,336
Nearly everything here comes
from university extension offices,

26
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,096
Illinois, Maryland,

27
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,136
New Hampshire,

28
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,396
Utah State, and Penn State.

29
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,056
Where the sources go quiet,

30
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:17,200
I'll tell you.

31
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:19,656
Let's start with the light,

32
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:21,880
because it explains everything else.

33
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:24,536
Picture a December afternoon.

34
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:27,036
The sun reaches the glass
for a little while,

35
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:30,916
slides across the sill, and then
it's gone behind the neighbor's roof.

36
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,636
The tray on that sill
barely got a day at all.

37
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:35,520
It just sits there in the dim.

38
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,856
Here's the thing about
plants and weak light.

39
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:40,196
They don't wilt first.

40
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:41,316
They stretch.

41
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:45,016
New Hampshire Extension points out that
a windowsill can be enough for seeds

42
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:46,776
that need light to sprout.

43
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:49,936
But once they sprout,
they stretch if the light is too weak,

44
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,840
just like any other seedling.

45
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:53,076
Tall, thin,

46
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:55,980
and reaching for the glass
like a kid on tiptoe.

47
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:58,736
That's the quiet sign.

48
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:02,796
If your seedlings are getting taller
and thinner while the leaves stay small,

49
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,536
they're asking for more light.

50
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:05,576
Don't panic.

51
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,979
It's an easy fix, and I'll
show you how in a few minutes.

52
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:12,056
And stretching is only the first warning.

53
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:13,996
For vegetables that make fruit,

54
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,956
New Hampshire says too
little light means tall,

55
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,336
spindly plants and failure
to flower and set fruit.

56
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:22,176
So the stretching comes first.

57
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,300
The flowers just never come.

58
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,356
So how much light does
a winter window give?

59
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:28,836
Here's the honest answer.

60
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:32,716
Illinois Extension's houseplant guide
calls a south-facing window the best

61
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,953
window option at about
three hundred foot candles.

62
00:02:36,037 --> 00:02:38,073
That's just a unit for measuring light,

63
00:02:38,157 --> 00:02:39,733
and you don't need to measure it.

64
00:02:39,817 --> 00:02:41,973
But that guide gives no winter hours,

65
00:02:42,057 --> 00:02:44,333
and neither does any other source I found.

66
00:02:44,417 --> 00:02:46,457
So I won't invent a number for you.

67
00:02:47,237 --> 00:02:49,653
What the universities do agree on is this.

68
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:52,373
New Hampshire says a bright, sunny window,

69
00:02:52,457 --> 00:02:54,153
best if it faces south,

70
00:02:54,237 --> 00:02:57,233
is almost a must for growing
vegetables indoors.

71
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:01,813
Illinois says lettuce and herbs need
six to eight hours of bright light.

72
00:03:01,897 --> 00:03:04,613
And Utah State says
to grow indoor tomatoes

73
00:03:04,697 --> 00:03:06,753
under a cool white fluorescent light.

74
00:03:06,837 --> 00:03:07,753
Different words,

75
00:03:07,837 --> 00:03:08,773
same point.

76
00:03:08,857 --> 00:03:10,853
Leaves usually forgive weak light.

77
00:03:10,937 --> 00:03:12,437
Fruit often doesn't.

78
00:03:13,137 --> 00:03:14,553
Here's why that matters.

79
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:17,013
It splits this list into two groups.

80
00:03:17,097 --> 00:03:19,333
The leafy crops can try your window.

81
00:03:19,417 --> 00:03:21,273
The fruiting crops need a lamp.

82
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:24,413
Know which group a plant
belongs to before you buy seeds,

83
00:03:24,497 --> 00:03:27,177
and you'll save yourself
a lot of wasted soil.

84
00:03:27,977 --> 00:03:29,473
Keep one number in your head,

85
00:03:29,557 --> 00:03:30,853
six to eight hours.

86
00:03:30,937 --> 00:03:32,637
We'll keep coming back to it.

87
00:03:33,437 --> 00:03:35,213
One more thing before we start.

88
00:03:35,297 --> 00:03:39,453
Two plants on this list need a stretch
of darkness at night to do their job,

89
00:03:39,537 --> 00:03:41,093
and only one of them does.

90
00:03:41,177 --> 00:03:43,213
Tomatoes and peppers don't agree on that,

91
00:03:43,297 --> 00:03:45,093
and it changes how you set a lamp.

92
00:03:45,177 --> 00:03:46,977
I'll get to it in a few minutes.

93
00:03:48,177 --> 00:03:50,613
Let's start with the fastest
thing on this list,

94
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:51,937
microgreens.

95
00:03:52,817 --> 00:03:54,953
Maryland Extension says microgreens are

96
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:57,653
ready seven to twenty-
one days after sowing,

97
00:03:57,737 --> 00:03:59,613
when they're two to three inches tall.

98
00:03:59,697 --> 00:04:01,353
That's the quickest harvest here,

99
00:04:01,437 --> 00:04:02,797
by a long way.

100
00:04:03,517 --> 00:04:05,693
You sow them thickly in a shallow tray,

101
00:04:05,777 --> 00:04:07,753
and you cut them with clean scissors

102
00:04:07,837 --> 00:04:11,333
just above the soil line once
the first true leaves appear.

103
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:13,793
Here's a small trick from the same guide.

104
00:04:13,877 --> 00:04:17,093
Keep the tray covered for a few
days after the seeds sprout

105
00:04:17,177 --> 00:04:18,913
and the stems come up longer,

106
00:04:18,997 --> 00:04:20,757
which makes cutting easier.

107
00:04:21,537 --> 00:04:23,373
Now, the honest limit.

108
00:04:23,457 --> 00:04:27,613
Maryland says light from a sunny
window may be enough in some cases,

109
00:04:27,697 --> 00:04:31,013
but microgreens generally
grow best under grow lights.

110
00:04:31,097 --> 00:04:36,073
It puts the total at about sixteen hours
of light a day from all sources combined.

111
00:04:36,157 --> 00:04:39,633
So think of microgreens as
a try the window first crop.

112
00:04:39,717 --> 00:04:42,537
If they start leaning
and thinning, add a lamp.

113
00:04:43,317 --> 00:04:46,273
It takes a minute to set
up and no special gear.

114
00:04:46,357 --> 00:04:48,213
All you need is a shallow tray,

115
00:04:48,297 --> 00:04:50,417
some potting mix, and seeds.

116
00:04:51,257 --> 00:04:52,593
Microgreens are quick,

117
00:04:52,677 --> 00:04:53,833
but they're tiny.

118
00:04:53,917 --> 00:04:56,573
If you want something you
can cut again and again,

119
00:04:56,657 --> 00:04:58,517
the next group is for you.

120
00:04:59,657 --> 00:05:02,733
Arugula, mustard greens, and Asian greens.

121
00:05:02,817 --> 00:05:04,293
These are baby greens.

122
00:05:04,377 --> 00:05:07,693
Maryland puts them at twenty-
one to forty days after sowing,

123
00:05:07,777 --> 00:05:09,833
when they're four to six inches tall.

124
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:11,293
And here's the part I like.

125
00:05:11,377 --> 00:05:14,617
They can regrow for one
or two more harvests.

126
00:05:15,457 --> 00:05:17,053
Here's why that's a bargain.

127
00:05:17,137 --> 00:05:18,313
You cut the leaves,

128
00:05:18,397 --> 00:05:19,533
you leave the plant,

129
00:05:19,617 --> 00:05:21,289
and it gives you another round.

130
00:05:21,373 --> 00:05:22,249
Same pot,

131
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:23,289
same seeds,

132
00:05:23,373 --> 00:05:24,553
more salad.

133
00:05:25,373 --> 00:05:27,049
And this is the forgiving group.

134
00:05:27,133 --> 00:05:29,149
Marilyn says lettuce, arugula,

135
00:05:29,233 --> 00:05:33,829
mustards, and Asian greens will grow
acceptably well in less than ideal light.

136
00:05:33,913 --> 00:05:34,949
Not great light.

137
00:05:35,033 --> 00:05:36,049
Acceptable.

138
00:05:36,133 --> 00:05:37,389
On a gray winter sill,

139
00:05:37,473 --> 00:05:39,413
acceptable is a big deal.

140
00:05:40,293 --> 00:05:44,489
So if you only plant one thing from this
video and your window isn’t perfect,

141
00:05:44,573 --> 00:05:45,893
make it this group.

142
00:05:46,973 --> 00:05:48,389
That’s two of seven.

143
00:05:48,473 --> 00:05:50,849
Next is the one everybody asks about:

144
00:05:50,933 --> 00:05:51,833
lettuce.

145
00:05:52,793 --> 00:05:57,309
Illinois Extension says loose leaf lettuce
can be harvested at about four weeks.

146
00:05:57,393 --> 00:05:58,929
That’s the whole timeline.

147
00:05:59,013 --> 00:06:01,089
Four weeks from seed to salad bowl,

148
00:06:01,173 --> 00:06:04,353
and that first crisp bite
feels like a small win.

149
00:06:05,213 --> 00:06:06,169
Now the catch,

150
00:06:06,253 --> 00:06:07,709
and it’s about your window.

151
00:06:07,793 --> 00:06:10,589
New Hampshire says lettuce
wants a very sunny spot.

152
00:06:10,673 --> 00:06:13,649
Illinois says it wants six
to eight hours of bright light.

153
00:06:13,733 --> 00:06:15,229
There’s that number again.

154
00:06:15,313 --> 00:06:17,029
So if your window faces south,

155
00:06:17,113 --> 00:06:18,549
try the sill first.

156
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:21,609
If it faces north or a big tree blocks it,

157
00:06:21,693 --> 00:06:23,669
plan on a lamp from day one.

158
00:06:23,753 --> 00:06:24,649
If that’s you,

159
00:06:24,733 --> 00:06:26,329
this next part is for you.

160
00:06:26,413 --> 00:06:28,009
The lamp isn’t a luxury.

161
00:06:28,093 --> 00:06:29,413
It’s the plan.

162
00:06:30,333 --> 00:06:32,129
Lettuce likes it cool too.

163
00:06:32,213 --> 00:06:35,309
Illinois says to keep the room
at seventy degrees or below,

164
00:06:35,393 --> 00:06:36,949
away from direct heat.

165
00:06:37,033 --> 00:06:41,493
That cool back bedroom nobody uses
might be the best salad bar in the house.

166
00:06:42,533 --> 00:06:45,929
New Hampshire names leaf lettuce
and a small butterhead called Tom

167
00:06:46,013 --> 00:06:48,133
Thumb as good ones to try.

168
00:06:48,273 --> 00:06:49,829
You don’t have to overthink it.

169
00:06:49,913 --> 00:06:52,833
Pick a leaf type and start with one tray.

170
00:06:53,753 --> 00:06:55,329
That’s three of seven.

171
00:06:55,413 --> 00:06:56,909
And when we get to the table,

172
00:06:56,993 --> 00:07:00,189
I’ll show you three places
where the universities disagree,

173
00:07:00,273 --> 00:07:02,989
including one about
washing your microgreens.

174
00:07:03,073 --> 00:07:03,949
But first,

175
00:07:04,033 --> 00:07:06,033
a pair that asks for patience.

176
00:07:07,593 --> 00:07:09,149
Chives and parsley.

177
00:07:09,233 --> 00:07:10,389
These are the slow ones,

178
00:07:10,473 --> 00:07:12,233
so let’s be upfront about it.

179
00:07:13,013 --> 00:07:17,369
New Hampshire Extension says chives take
about twelve weeks from seed to the first

180
00:07:17,453 --> 00:07:18,293
cutting.

181
00:07:18,473 --> 00:07:20,769
Parsley takes ten to twelve weeks.

182
00:07:20,853 --> 00:07:22,529
That’s nearly three months.

183
00:07:22,613 --> 00:07:24,929
So this isn’t a salad next week crop.

184
00:07:25,013 --> 00:07:28,313
It’s a plant it now,
snip it in the dead of winter crop.

185
00:07:29,513 --> 00:07:33,069
The good news is that chives prefer
cool conditions with good light,

186
00:07:33,153 --> 00:07:36,653
and New Hampshire says they grow
quite well on a kitchen windowsill.

187
00:07:36,893 --> 00:07:39,129
Plant the seeds in a six-inch pot.

188
00:07:39,213 --> 00:07:40,049
For parsley,

189
00:07:40,133 --> 00:07:42,549
one vigorous plant per pot is plenty.

190
00:07:42,633 --> 00:07:45,469
And Illinois puts herbs
in the same bracket as lettuce,

191
00:07:45,553 --> 00:07:47,633
six to eight hours of bright light.

192
00:07:48,453 --> 00:07:49,689
Here’s the trade.

193
00:07:49,773 --> 00:07:50,729
You wait longer,

194
00:07:50,813 --> 00:07:55,253
and in return you get a pot you can snip
a few sprigs from whenever you’re cooking.

195
00:07:55,373 --> 00:07:57,109
Patience pays on this one,

196
00:07:57,193 --> 00:08:00,653
and there’s real comfort
in a green pot waiting on the sill.

197
00:08:01,393 --> 00:08:02,709
That’s four of seven.

198
00:08:02,793 --> 00:08:05,893
The next one is where
the sources get very quiet.

199
00:08:07,053 --> 00:08:08,129
Radishes.

200
00:08:08,213 --> 00:08:11,325
Here’s exactly what
the sources say and no more.

201
00:08:12,126 --> 00:08:16,682
New Hampshire Extension calls radishes
a quick-growing crop for indoors.

202
00:08:16,766 --> 00:08:20,062
Scatter the seeds on moist
soil in a six-inch pot.

203
00:08:20,146 --> 00:08:23,722
It adds that radishes must be grown
very rapidly if they're to be crisp

204
00:08:23,806 --> 00:08:25,006
and succulent.

205
00:08:25,886 --> 00:08:27,582
And here's what I could not find.

206
00:08:27,666 --> 00:08:32,182
No extension source I found gives
a number of days or hours of light

207
00:08:32,266 --> 00:08:34,262
for radishes grown indoors.

208
00:08:34,346 --> 00:08:37,042
Some videos online give exact figures.

209
00:08:37,126 --> 00:08:39,582
I can't back them up,
so I won't repeat them.

210
00:08:39,666 --> 00:08:41,502
Treat radish as a test.

211
00:08:41,586 --> 00:08:43,782
One small pot, your brightest sill,

212
00:08:43,866 --> 00:08:45,246
and see what you get.

213
00:08:45,906 --> 00:08:47,022
That's five.

214
00:08:47,106 --> 00:08:48,522
The easy group is done,

215
00:08:48,606 --> 00:08:52,342
and so far you haven't needed
to buy anything but seeds and a tray.

216
00:08:52,426 --> 00:08:54,826
The next two are where the lamp comes in.

217
00:08:55,926 --> 00:08:57,342
Tomatoes first.

218
00:08:57,426 --> 00:08:59,686
The small ones can work indoors.

219
00:09:00,526 --> 00:09:05,422
New Hampshire Extension names Tiny Tim,
Small Fry, and a paste tomato called Roma

220
00:09:05,506 --> 00:09:08,706
as ones that can be raised
quite satisfactorily at home.

221
00:09:08,806 --> 00:09:11,865
Tiny Tim stays around twelve
to fifteen inches tall.

222
00:09:11,966 --> 00:09:14,002
Small Fry is closer to three feet,

223
00:09:14,086 --> 00:09:16,442
and New Hampshire suggests
giving it more room,

224
00:09:16,526 --> 00:09:17,806
like a sunroom.

225
00:09:17,906 --> 00:09:20,422
Utah State adds more names to look for.

226
00:09:20,506 --> 00:09:24,062
Tiny Tim, Micro Tom, Terenzo, and Lozano.

227
00:09:24,146 --> 00:09:27,306
For the smallest kinds,
a one-gallon pot is enough.

228
00:09:28,186 --> 00:09:29,406
Now the light.

229
00:09:29,526 --> 00:09:32,862
New Hampshire says fruiting
vegetables like tomatoes

230
00:09:32,946 --> 00:09:35,542
need supplemental light
during the winter months.

231
00:09:35,626 --> 00:09:40,406
Without it, you get tall, spindly
plants that fail to flower and set fruit.

232
00:09:40,506 --> 00:09:44,602
Utah State says to grow them under
a cool white fluorescent light

233
00:09:44,686 --> 00:09:48,982
with the tops of the plants staying
within three to six inches of the bulbs.

234
00:09:49,066 --> 00:09:51,386
A south or west window helps too.

235
00:09:52,186 --> 00:09:54,142
And here's the surprise I promised.

236
00:09:54,226 --> 00:09:55,962
Tomatoes need darkness.

237
00:09:56,046 --> 00:09:59,602
New Hampshire's lighting guide says
tomatoes actually need about six hours

238
00:09:59,686 --> 00:10:00,822
of dark to fruit.

239
00:10:00,906 --> 00:10:04,522
It adds that some tomatoes are being
bred to handle round-the-clock light.

240
00:10:04,606 --> 00:10:06,182
But for what you'd plant at home,

241
00:10:06,266 --> 00:10:07,762
plan on that dark break.

242
00:10:07,846 --> 00:10:09,102
Call it beauty sleep.

243
00:10:09,186 --> 00:10:12,166
Peppers, as we'll see
in a second, are different.

244
00:10:12,906 --> 00:10:14,362
Here's why this matters.

245
00:10:14,446 --> 00:10:17,042
A lamp that never turns
off sounds generous,

246
00:10:17,126 --> 00:10:19,502
but for a tomato, it can work against you.

247
00:10:19,586 --> 00:10:24,002
So use a timer and let the lamp go off
for at least six hours every night.

248
00:10:24,086 --> 00:10:25,006
That's it.

249
00:10:25,846 --> 00:10:27,186
Two cautions.

250
00:10:27,346 --> 00:10:31,582
Utah State says temperatures below
fifty degrees can harm tomatoes,

251
00:10:31,666 --> 00:10:35,002
and temperatures above ninety
may keep fruit from setting.

252
00:10:35,086 --> 00:10:39,786
And New Hampshire says to watch for
whiteflies and aphids on indoor tomatoes.

253
00:10:40,686 --> 00:10:42,126
Then there's the wait.

254
00:10:42,226 --> 00:10:45,862
New Hampshire says tomatoes and peppers
are ready to harvest about ten weeks

255
00:10:45,946 --> 00:10:47,102
after planting.

256
00:10:47,186 --> 00:10:50,002
Ten weeks of lamp, timer, and patience.

257
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:51,762
If that doesn't sound like you,

258
00:10:51,846 --> 00:10:53,062
leave this one out.

259
00:10:53,146 --> 00:10:54,826
Nobody's keeping score.

260
00:10:56,046 --> 00:10:57,662
Peppers are the other lamp crop.

261
00:10:57,746 --> 00:11:01,602
New Hampshire says small-fruited
peppers can be grown as indoor plants.

262
00:11:01,686 --> 00:11:02,882
And like tomatoes,

263
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:05,022
they need warm, bright conditions.

264
00:11:05,106 --> 00:11:06,962
Same lamp rule applies.

265
00:11:07,046 --> 00:11:08,622
Supplemental light in winter,

266
00:11:08,706 --> 00:11:11,526
or you get tall, thin plants and no fruit.

267
00:11:12,326 --> 00:11:13,562
But here's the difference.

268
00:11:13,646 --> 00:11:15,882
Tomatoes need that stretch of dark.

269
00:11:15,966 --> 00:11:18,966
New Hampshire's lighting
guide says peppers do not.

270
00:11:19,066 --> 00:11:21,866
So peppers don't need the same dark break.

271
00:11:22,966 --> 00:11:25,002
Harvest comes in at about ten weeks,

272
00:11:25,086 --> 00:11:26,382
same as tomatoes.

273
00:11:26,466 --> 00:11:27,842
And the same two pests,

274
00:11:27,926 --> 00:11:29,342
whiteflies and aphids,

275
00:11:29,426 --> 00:11:30,566
may show up.

276
00:11:31,346 --> 00:11:32,602
I'll say this plainly.

277
00:11:32,686 --> 00:11:36,502
The sources I found don't name
pepper varieties for indoor growing.

278
00:11:36,586 --> 00:11:40,686
So choose a small-fruited type
and check the seed packet for size.

279
00:11:41,826 --> 00:11:42,822
That's all seven.

280
00:11:42,906 --> 00:11:44,846
Now let's put them in one place.

281
00:11:45,986 --> 00:11:47,042
Here's the table.

282
00:11:47,126 --> 00:11:49,342
Seven crops side by side.

283
00:11:49,426 --> 00:11:53,662
It's the one thing in this video you
won't find in any single university guide

284
00:11:53,746 --> 00:11:56,186
because I lined up their numbers myself.

285
00:11:57,066 --> 00:11:58,602
Leafy crops first.

286
00:11:58,686 --> 00:11:59,742
Microgreens,

287
00:11:59,826 --> 00:12:01,562
seven to twenty-one days.

288
00:12:01,646 --> 00:12:03,702
Light from a sunny window may do.

289
00:12:03,786 --> 00:12:05,102
A lamp is better.

290
00:12:05,186 --> 00:12:06,262
Baby greens,

291
00:12:06,346 --> 00:12:07,902
twenty-one to forty days,

292
00:12:07,986 --> 00:12:09,946
and they tolerate weaker light.

293
00:12:10,046 --> 00:12:13,842
Lettuce, about four weeks
in a very sunny spot.

294
00:12:13,926 --> 00:12:15,122
Chives and parsley,

295
00:12:15,206 --> 00:12:18,642
ten to twelve weeks with six
to eight hours of bright light.

296
00:12:18,726 --> 00:12:21,242
Radish, and the sources don't say.

297
00:12:21,326 --> 00:12:22,642
Then the fruit crops.

298
00:12:22,726 --> 00:12:23,982
Tomatoes and peppers,

299
00:12:24,066 --> 00:12:25,162
about ten weeks

300
00:12:25,246 --> 00:12:27,286
with supplemental light in winter.

301
00:12:28,246 --> 00:12:30,682
Now for the part I find most interesting.

302
00:12:30,766 --> 00:12:34,046
Here are three places where
the universities disagree.

303
00:12:34,926 --> 00:12:36,882
First, how many hours of light?

304
00:12:36,966 --> 00:12:40,602
Illinois says lettuce and herbs need
six to eight hours of bright light.

305
00:12:40,686 --> 00:12:44,922
Maryland says the total should be about
sixteen hours a day from all sources.

306
00:12:45,006 --> 00:12:47,342
Those look different,
but read the wording.

307
00:12:47,426 --> 00:12:49,642
One is about the bright
light a plant needs.

308
00:12:49,726 --> 00:12:52,162
The other counts every
hour from every source,

309
00:12:52,246 --> 00:12:53,526
lamp included.

310
00:12:54,406 --> 00:12:56,622
Second, how far should the lamp be?

311
00:12:56,706 --> 00:12:58,822
Illinois says six to twelve inches.

312
00:12:58,906 --> 00:13:00,882
New Hampshire says four to six.

313
00:13:00,966 --> 00:13:03,662
Utah State says three to six for tomatoes.

314
00:13:03,746 --> 00:13:06,162
I couldn't find a source
that explains the gap.

315
00:13:06,246 --> 00:13:08,462
So here's the rule that
works with all of them.

316
00:13:08,546 --> 00:13:10,162
Start where your guide says.

317
00:13:10,246 --> 00:13:11,462
If the plants stretch,

318
00:13:11,546 --> 00:13:12,622
lower the lamp.

319
00:13:12,706 --> 00:13:14,362
If they burn, raise it.

320
00:13:14,446 --> 00:13:16,166
That's straight from New Hampshire.

321
00:13:17,206 --> 00:13:20,506
Third, should you wash
microgreens after cutting?

322
00:13:20,606 --> 00:13:25,142
Maryland says to rinse them with water,
pat them dry, and use them right away,

323
00:13:25,226 --> 00:13:27,851
or keep them in the fridge
for up to ten days.

324
00:13:27,951 --> 00:13:31,807
Penn State says post-harvest washing
is not recommended unless absolutely

325
00:13:31,891 --> 00:13:35,111
necessary because it can
spread contamination.

326
00:13:35,251 --> 00:13:36,167
One thing to know:

327
00:13:36,251 --> 00:13:40,287
Penn State's guidance is framed around
commercial microgreens producers.

328
00:13:40,371 --> 00:13:42,287
Both say to use clean water.

329
00:13:42,371 --> 00:13:43,707
So if you grow at home,

330
00:13:43,791 --> 00:13:47,751
ask your local extension office
and use clean water either way.

331
00:13:48,551 --> 00:13:49,527
That's the table.

332
00:13:49,611 --> 00:13:50,807
Now, lamps.

333
00:13:50,891 --> 00:13:52,571
And you don't need a fancy one.

334
00:13:53,391 --> 00:13:56,027
Illinois Extension
describes a simple setup:

335
00:13:56,111 --> 00:13:59,107
two forty-watt cool
white fluorescent tubes

336
00:13:59,191 --> 00:14:01,447
six to twelve inches above the plants,

337
00:14:01,531 --> 00:14:04,127
running fourteen to sixteen hours a day.

338
00:14:04,211 --> 00:14:05,207
In another guide,

339
00:14:05,291 --> 00:14:08,507
Illinois says it prefers
T5 fluorescent tubes

340
00:14:08,591 --> 00:14:11,107
and calls older types less efficient.

341
00:14:11,191 --> 00:14:15,267
And New Hampshire says LED
shop lamps will work fine.

342
00:14:15,351 --> 00:14:18,087
Maryland adds one more point for LEDs.

343
00:14:18,171 --> 00:14:22,091
They use less energy than fluorescent
lights for the same amount of light.

344
00:14:22,751 --> 00:14:24,467
Here's where the experts agree.

345
00:14:24,551 --> 00:14:27,207
Illinois, New Hampshire, and Utah State

346
00:14:27,291 --> 00:14:28,327
word it differently,

347
00:14:28,411 --> 00:14:30,371
but they land on one idea:

348
00:14:30,511 --> 00:14:33,427
keep the light close and let it run long.

349
00:14:33,511 --> 00:14:36,527
Close means a few inches
from the tops of the plants.

350
00:14:36,611 --> 00:14:38,571
Long means most of the day.

351
00:14:38,691 --> 00:14:41,067
Then let the plant tell you what it wants.

352
00:14:41,151 --> 00:14:42,167
If it stretches,

353
00:14:42,251 --> 00:14:44,327
lower the lamp or run it longer.

354
00:14:44,411 --> 00:14:46,327
If the leaves burn, raise it.

355
00:14:46,411 --> 00:14:47,807
That's the whole system.

356
00:14:47,891 --> 00:14:49,851
Nudge it like tuning a radio.

357
00:14:50,751 --> 00:14:51,687
Use a timer.

358
00:14:51,771 --> 00:14:55,107
Illinois uses an automatic
timer to set its lamp hours.

359
00:14:55,191 --> 00:14:56,627
It takes a minute to set,

360
00:14:56,711 --> 00:14:58,671
and then you never have to remember.

361
00:14:59,511 --> 00:15:00,587
How many hours?

362
00:15:00,671 --> 00:15:03,587
Illinois says sixteen
hours of supplemental light,

363
00:15:03,671 --> 00:15:05,387
with eighteen as the maximum,

364
00:15:05,471 --> 00:15:06,887
but that's for seedlings.

365
00:15:06,971 --> 00:15:08,067
And for tomatoes,

366
00:15:08,151 --> 00:15:10,067
remember the six hours of dark.

367
00:15:10,151 --> 00:15:12,431
So use the guide that matches your plant.

368
00:15:13,071 --> 00:15:14,807
There's one more rule of thumb.

369
00:15:14,891 --> 00:15:19,627
Illinois suggests about two hours of
lamplight for every hour of natural light.

370
00:15:19,711 --> 00:15:22,747
That comes from one short
guide from twenty seventeen,

371
00:15:22,831 --> 00:15:24,147
and it's a rule of thumb,

372
00:15:24,231 --> 00:15:25,707
not a measured law.

373
00:15:25,791 --> 00:15:27,511
Use it as a starting point.

374
00:15:28,451 --> 00:15:30,387
Two things I'm leaving out on purpose:

375
00:15:30,471 --> 00:15:34,867
prices, because I haven't found a neutral
source for what a lamp should cost,

376
00:15:34,951 --> 00:15:36,127
and brand names,

377
00:15:36,211 --> 00:15:39,091
because none of the extension
guides recommend one.

378
00:15:40,191 --> 00:15:42,627
Three problems come up again and again,

379
00:15:42,711 --> 00:15:45,571
and every one has a calm, simple fix.

380
00:15:46,431 --> 00:15:47,547
Problem one:

381
00:15:47,631 --> 00:15:48,507
stretching.

382
00:15:48,591 --> 00:15:50,067
We covered the cause.

383
00:15:50,151 --> 00:15:52,027
The fix, from New Hampshire,

384
00:15:52,111 --> 00:15:53,327
is to lower the lamp,

385
00:15:53,411 --> 00:15:54,287
run it longer,

386
00:15:54,371 --> 00:15:55,391
or both.

387
00:15:56,611 --> 00:15:57,567
Problem two:

388
00:15:57,651 --> 00:15:58,987
mold on the tray.

389
00:15:59,071 --> 00:16:02,147
Maryland says to make sure
there's good air circulation

390
00:16:02,231 --> 00:16:06,979
and suggests a gentle fan breeze
across dense plantings like microgreens.

391
00:16:07,063 --> 00:16:09,423
Wash trays before you reuse them.

392
00:16:10,263 --> 00:16:11,459
Problem three:

393
00:16:11,543 --> 00:16:12,859
tiny pests.

394
00:16:12,943 --> 00:16:14,979
New Hampshire says whiteflies and aphids

395
00:16:15,063 --> 00:16:17,699
can show up on indoor
tomatoes and peppers.

396
00:16:17,783 --> 00:16:20,239
Yellow sticky traps catch whiteflies.

397
00:16:20,323 --> 00:16:23,159
For aphids, it says insecticidal soap

398
00:16:23,243 --> 00:16:26,459
or another product approved
for vegetables can be used.

399
00:16:26,543 --> 00:16:28,259
If you use anything like that,

400
00:16:28,343 --> 00:16:29,499
read the label.

401
00:16:29,583 --> 00:16:31,759
Make sure it's approved for vegetables.

402
00:16:31,843 --> 00:16:33,139
Follow it closely,

403
00:16:33,223 --> 00:16:35,439
including any wait before harvest,

404
00:16:35,523 --> 00:16:38,179
and store it out
of reach of kids and pets.

405
00:16:38,263 --> 00:16:40,059
And if you have a dog or a cat,

406
00:16:40,143 --> 00:16:44,663
check each plant's name against the ASPCA
list before it goes on a low shelf.

407
00:16:45,483 --> 00:16:48,979
And one word on safety, because it
comes up a lot. Some people want

408
00:16:49,063 --> 00:16:52,619
to grow their own greens because
they worry about store-bought ones.

409
00:16:52,703 --> 00:16:55,319
Nothing I found says homegrown is safer.

410
00:16:55,403 --> 00:16:59,119
Penn State's guidance for microgreens
is about keeping things clean,

411
00:16:59,203 --> 00:17:01,279
using water that's free of E. coli,

412
00:17:01,363 --> 00:17:03,159
and not spreading contamination.

413
00:17:03,243 --> 00:17:04,779
So grow it, enjoy it,

414
00:17:04,863 --> 00:17:07,363
and keep your trays and your water clean.

415
00:17:08,503 --> 00:17:11,503
Now let's turn all this
into a gentle little plan.

416
00:17:12,323 --> 00:17:13,259
Week one,

417
00:17:13,343 --> 00:17:16,259
sow microgreens
and a tray of leaf lettuce.

418
00:17:16,343 --> 00:17:18,579
Set up a lamp if your window is weak.

419
00:17:18,663 --> 00:17:20,379
If you want tomatoes or peppers,

420
00:17:20,463 --> 00:17:21,839
start those now too,

421
00:17:21,923 --> 00:17:23,983
because they take about ten weeks.

422
00:17:24,883 --> 00:17:26,383
Weeks two and three,

423
00:17:26,483 --> 00:17:29,819
microgreens are ready seven
to twenty-one days after sowing.

424
00:17:29,903 --> 00:17:31,683
Cut them with clean scissors.

425
00:17:32,663 --> 00:17:33,659
Week four,

426
00:17:33,743 --> 00:17:36,579
loose leaf lettuce is
ready at about four weeks.

427
00:17:36,663 --> 00:17:38,799
Chives and parsley keep growing.

428
00:17:38,883 --> 00:17:40,883
They take ten to twelve weeks.

429
00:17:41,783 --> 00:17:43,919
And here's the one thing to do tomorrow.

430
00:17:44,003 --> 00:17:46,259
Tape a sheet of paper next to the window.

431
00:17:46,343 --> 00:17:47,219
Each hour,

432
00:17:47,303 --> 00:17:49,259
write sun or no sun.

433
00:17:49,343 --> 00:17:50,959
Do it for a single day.

434
00:17:51,043 --> 00:17:52,339
That's your own number.

435
00:17:52,423 --> 00:17:54,319
Compare it to six to eight hours

436
00:17:54,403 --> 00:17:57,983
and you'll know which group
you belong to. No tools needed.

437
00:17:58,783 --> 00:18:00,079
By the end of week three,

438
00:18:00,163 --> 00:18:04,219
you could be snipping fresh microgreens
off the sill in the middle of winter.

439
00:18:04,303 --> 00:18:06,439
Outside, everything is gray.

440
00:18:06,523 --> 00:18:08,663
Inside, your tray is green.

441
00:18:09,523 --> 00:18:13,579
If you're the kind of gardener who checks
the sky before you check the forecast,

442
00:18:13,663 --> 00:18:14,983
you belong here.

443
00:18:15,743 --> 00:18:17,819
Which way does your kitchen window face

444
00:18:17,903 --> 00:18:20,659
and how many hours of sun
does it get in winter?

445
00:18:20,743 --> 00:18:22,243
Tell me in the comments.

446
00:18:23,063 --> 00:18:24,979
Pick one. Not all seven.

447
00:18:25,063 --> 00:18:26,083
One tray.

448
00:18:26,923 --> 00:18:30,019
Grow a little, save a little,
and have some fun out there.

449
00:18:30,103 --> 00:18:31,144
Happy gardening.

